Chapter: VehicleLimitsSpec
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter VehicleLimits
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 VehicleLimits, and the table lookup whose miss is indistinguishable from a hit.

 `turn-speed` is a six-entry table read by rounded degrees, with a fallback. The
 fallback is 0.222, WHICH IS ALSO THE 50-DEGREE ENTRY. So a lookup that misses
 returns exactly what a 50-degree corner returns, and no caller, no check and no
 line in this file can tell the two apart by value. That is worth writing down
 rather than discovering later: the table has no observable "not found".

 WHAT THIS FILE CAN DO ABOUT IT is grade the boundary, which is where a miss
 actually gets produced. The lookup rounds first -- ties away from zero -- so
 14.5 degrees is a 15-degree corner at 1.297 and 14.4 degrees is a miss at
 0.222. Those two inputs are a tenth of a degree apart and their answers differ
 by a factor of six. A table read on a Real without that rounding would put the
 boundary somewhere else entirely, and only a pair straddling it would notice.

 THE NEGATIVE ANGLE IS GRADED AND IS UNREACHABLE TODAY. `turn-speed (-15 deg)`
 rounds to -15, matches nothing, and falls through to 0.222 rather than
 answering the 15-degree entry. No caller can reach it: World sets
 `exit-angle = real-abs c.turn-deg * deg`, so every angle handed to this
 function is non-negative. It is graded for the same reason NumSpec grades a
 domain no caller produces -- the asymmetry is a property of the function, the
 caller's `real-abs` is what currently hides it, and a spec that only tested
 what today's callers pass would report the function as symmetric.

 WHERE THE EXPECTED VALUES COME FROM: the table in the chapter, which is the
 table from the TypeScript, plus the rounding rule. The speeds are literals in
 the source and there is nothing to derive; what is derived is WHICH entry each
 input selects, and that is the only thing here an implementation could get
 wrong.

 THE ROUND TRIP THROUGH RADIANS IS PART OF THE TEST. Inputs are given in degrees
 and multiplied by `deg`, exactly as World does, so the lookup has to recover
 the degree count from a radian value that is not exact. `round-real` is what
 makes that safe, and grading the tabulated angles at 0.0 asserts it.

 We say:

Section: Degrees In

  to-rad : Real -> Real
  to-rad (d) = d * deg

Section: The Table

 All six entries, in table order. 30 is in the table and no segment on this
 route turns by it; it is graded because the table has it, not because the game
 reaches it.

  table-deg : List Real
  table-deg = [15.0, 20.0, 30.0, 50.0, 70.0, 80.0]

  table-want : List Real
  table-want = [1.297, 0.840, 0.461, 0.222, 0.139, 0.117]

Section: The Boundary And The Miss

 14.5 rounds away from zero to 15 and hits; 14.4 rounds to 14 and misses. 30.5
 rounds to 31 and misses although it sits between two entries. 0.0 is the
 terminus's angle and misses -- both callers guard it with `terminates` and
 never ask, which is why the miss has never mattered. -15.0 is the asymmetry.

  edge-deg : List Real
  edge-deg = [14.5, 14.4, 15.4, 15.5, 30.5, 0.0, -15.0, -50.0]

  edge-want : List Real
  edge-want = [1.297, 0.222, 1.297, 0.222, 0.222, 0.222, 0.222, 0.222]

Section: The Envelope

 Three pinned constants. The truck expresses its whole envelope as 1.1 times two
 of these, so a change here moves both vehicles.

  env-got : List Real
  env-got = [a-accel, v-max, approach-intersection-dist]

  env-want : List Real
  env-want = [0.010, 2.5, 60.0]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "v-table" (list-map turn-speed (list-map to-rad table-deg)) table-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "v-edge " (list-map turn-speed (list-map to-rad edge-deg)) edge-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "v-env  " env-got env-want 0.0)
  end
